// THE SHIPPED CLOUD MEDIUM — what a cloud IS at a point in space when nobody has authored anything.
//
// THIS FILE IS NEVER SUBSTITUTED, and that is the whole reason it is separate from
// Generated/CloudMedium.glslh. That file is the SEAM: a cloud material replaces its bytes with an
// authored graph's, so nothing in it survives into an authored sky. These functions have to survive,
// because an authored medium is almost always a MODIFICATION of the shipped one — "the same clouds,
// warmer at the edges", "the same clouds, half as dense over there" — and a graph that had to rebuild
// the erosion chain by hand to say that would be a graph nobody could use. They are also what makes
// the acceptance test of the whole mechanism expressible: a Volume Output node with nothing wired into
// it compiles to five calls into this file, so "a graph that changes nothing" is a thing that can be
// byte-for-byte identical to no graph at all, rather than a thing to argue about.
//
// EVERY DEFAULT IS EITHER THE CHAIN THAT ALWAYS STOOD HERE OR AN IDENTITY. The density chain below moved
// out of Common/CloudField.glslh UNCHANGED; the other four hand back what the caller already had, which
// is what lets the compiler fold them away entirely. That is why the shipped frame costs exactly what it
// cost before this seam existed.
//
// UNITS: kilometres. The layer's own maths is kilometre-scaled even though the world is centimetres.

#ifndef CLOUD_MEDIUM_DEFAULT_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_MEDIUM_DEFAULT_GLSLH


// The sample position in the frame the WEATHER moves in — the layer's accumulated wind displacement
// taken off the world position.
//
// A FUNCTION AND NOT TWO COPIES OF ONE SUBTRACTION. It stood written out in both places below, and the
// pair is the shape this subsystem has already paid for once: a graph that wants structure to travel
// with the weather and the shipped erosion that already does must be reading the SAME frame, or an
// authored medium built on `WindPositionKm` drifts against the erosion it is modifying.
vec3 CloudDefaultWindPositionKm( CloudFieldParams params, vec3 positionKm )
{
    return vec3( positionKm.x - params.WindOffsetKm.x, positionKm.y - params.WindOffsetKm.y,
                 positionKm.z - params.WindOffsetKm.z );
}

// WHERE THE SHIPPED EROSION READS ITS VOLUME — the texture coordinate, in tiles, that the density chain
// below hands CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE.
//
// It is a function because it is the graph's starting point and not only the chain's. "The same volume,
// eroded differently" is the whole reason a medium can name a `.dcnv` at all, and the cheapest form of
// "differently" is a different coordinate — so the Cloud Noise Volume node's unwired coordinate is this
// expression, exactly as an unwired Volume Output pin is the shipped default. An artist reproducing it
// by hand would need three frequency constants the palette does not expose and a reciprocal it has no
// node for.
//
// ANISOTROPIC, and it was not. The erosion was the one octave here still sampled as a cube, which gives a
// torn cloud edge the proportions of gravel; the graph has anisotropy on all three of its octaves. This
// one takes the INVERTED triple — vertical period longer than horizontal — so the wisps it cuts are
// taller than they are wide, which is what a shred of cloud being carried upward looks like.
vec3 CloudDefaultNoiseCoordinate( CloudFieldParams params, vec3 positionKm )
{
    vec3  windPos = CloudDefaultWindPositionKm( params, positionKm );
    float tile    = max( params.DetailTileKm, 1e-4f );
    return vec3( windPos.x * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_X / tile, windPos.y * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Y / tile,
                 windPos.z * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Z / tile );
}

// A graph's slot number, made safe to hand CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE.
//
// THE PALETTE HAS ONE NUMERIC PIN TYPE AND IT IS float, so a slot an artist computed arrives as one —
// and the fetch is a four-way select on an int. Rounding rather than truncating is what makes a value
// carried through a `mix` land on the slot the author meant instead of the one below it; the clamp is
// what stops a slot outside the layer's four from silently reading volume 0, which is the fetch's own
// `else` branch and reads as "my second cloud type is not eroding".
int CloudNoiseSlotOf( float slot )
{
    return int( clamp( floor( slot + 0.5f ), 0.0f, float( CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS - 1 ) ) );
}

// The up-rez: turn the low-frequency profile into something with a cloud's edge.
//
// The erosion is a REMAP, not a subtraction (deck p.120, `ValueErosion`). The difference shows at the
// core: subtracting noise thins a dense cloud as much as a wispy one, so a cumulus turns translucent in
// the middle, while remapping leaves anything already deep inside the body at full density and only cuts
// where the profile was already falling off.
//
// `DetailType` selects between the volume's two CHARACTERS — wispy and billowy — and each of them is
// itself a frequency blend driven by the profile. That is the deck's composite on p.122 in full rather
// than the two-channel half of it that stood here, and the difference is that the erosion's frequency now
// follows how deep inside the body the sample is instead of being constant through the whole cloud.
float CloudDefaultDensity( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm )
{
    if ( field.Profile <= 0.0f )
        return 0.0f;

    vec3 detailPos = CloudDefaultNoiseCoordinate( params, positionKm );

    // THE VOLUME IS THE WINNING SPECIES', not the layer's. Until this argument existed there was one
    // `sampler3D` on the march and a Cirrus standing beside a Cumulus was cut from the cumulus' volume —
    // the last recorded debt of the cloud programme, and a dead setting in three of a layer's four slots.
    vec4 noise = CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE( field.NoiseSlot, detailPos );

    // THE DECK'S OWN COMPOSITE (p.122), and it uses all four channels rather than two of them.
    //
    // Each character of erosion is a blend of ITS OWN pair across frequency, and the blend is driven by the
    // profile — deep inside the body the coarse octave, out at the edge the fine one, which is the
    // frequency-follows-depth relation the deck states on p.101 and p.105. The wispy end blends linearly;
    // the billowy end blends on the fourth root, so a billowy cloud reaches its fine octave much earlier
    // and its edge breaks into lobes rather than fading into one smooth frequency.
    //
    // Only THEN do the two characters mix, on DetailType. Written the other way round — pick a channel by
    // type and never blend by depth — the erosion has one frequency everywhere inside a cloud, which is
    // what the two-channel version this replaces did.
    float wispy   = mix( noise.x, noise.y, field.Profile );
    float billowy = mix( noise.z, noise.w, pow( field.Profile, 0.25f ) );

    float detailField = clamp( mix( wispy, billowy, clamp( field.DetailType, 0.0f, 1.0f ) ), 0.0f, 1.0f );

    // THE EROSION IS WEIGHTED BY DEPTH, and this is the difference between clouds and fog.
    //
    // Applied uniformly, the erosion subtracts as much from the core of a cloud as from its edge — and
    // since the profile is only high in a thin band anyway, a strength of 0.5 removes most of the layer
    // and what survives is a translucent veil. That is not a tuning failure: a cut of fixed depth into a
    // field whose typical value is small removes the field.
    //
    // Weighting by (1 - Profile) puts the whole cut where the cloud is already ending. The core, which is
    // deep inside the body, keeps its density and stays solid; the edge, which is barely inside it, is
    // eaten into billows and wisps. It is the same principle the deck states as a frequency blend driven
    // by the dimensional profile (p.101, p.105) — the profile decides how much detail belongs here — and
    // it is why that field means "depth inside the body" rather than "density".
    // THE DEPTH OF THE CUT IS THE WINNING SPECIES', multiplied into the layer's own strength here rather
    // than folded together on the CPU as it was while a layer had one kind of cloud in it. A lenticular
    // has to stay a smooth lens and a cirrus has to stay shredded when the two are in the same sky, and a
    // single product formed once could only describe one of them.
    // THE FACTOR IS BOUNDED ON BOTH SIDES AND NOT ONLY BELOW. `max(x, 0)` leaves `+inf` intact, and this
    // product's other operand is a slider whose zero position is the whole subject of the test below it —
    // so the unbounded version turns "erosion off" into a NaN density for every sample in the body. See
    // the note on CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR_MAX: on every value either producer can legally carry, this bound
    // is the identity.
    float edgeWeight = 1.0f - clamp( field.Profile, 0.0f, 1.0f );
    float erosion    = detailField *
                    clamp( params.DetailStrength * CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( field.DetailFactor ), 0.0f, 1.0f ) *
                    edgeWeight;

    float density = CloudRemap( field.Profile, erosion, 1.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f );

    return clamp( density * field.DensityScale, 0.0f, 1.0f );
}

// How opaque the medium is, as a MULTIPLE of the layer's per-kilometre extinction.
//
// The winning species' own factor, which is where it has always come from: a shadow ray out of a
// congestus tower crosses the stratocumulus deck below it, and one extinction for the whole shell would
// shade the tower with the tower's own material. A graph replaces the MULTIPLE, never the layer's
// number — the layer's is a material parameter and stays authored where it is.
float CloudDefaultExtinctionFactor( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm )
{
    return field.ExtinctionFactor;
}

// The fraction of extinguished light that is SCATTERED rather than absorbed, per colour.
//
// @p materialAlbedo is the layer's own Scattering Albedo, already clamped by the caller. Handing it back
// unchanged is what makes the default free: the two programs that light clouds fold this call away
// entirely, and the two that do not light them never call it.
vec3 CloudDefaultAlbedo( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm, vec3 materialAlbedo )
{
    return materialAlbedo;
}

// Radiance the medium ADDS, per kilometre of march. Lightning inside a cell, a sodium-lit underside, a
// pyrocumulus.
//
// ZERO, AND AS A LITERAL. There is no emissive material parameter to default to and there deliberately
// is not one: a constant over a whole layer is the one thing a volume emission is least useful as, and it
// would have cost two more unread slots in the parameter block (O1_DESIGN §11.5). Emission enters the
// engine as a graph PIN or not at all, and until a graph supplies one this constant is removed by the
// compiler along with the multiply-add that consumes it.
vec3 CloudDefaultEmissive( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm )
{
    return vec3( 0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f );
}

// How much of the sky term reaches this sample, as a multiplier in [0,1].
//
// @p ambientOcclusion is what the layer already decided — the sky occlusion volume's answer when that
// pass is on, the sample's own depth inside its body when it is off — scaled by the material's Ambient
// Occlusion Strength. Returning it untouched leaves the choice of occluder with the component and the
// strength with the material; a graph multiplies its own term into theirs rather than replacing a pass it
// cannot see.
float CloudDefaultOcclusion( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm,
                             float ambientOcclusion )
{
    return ambientOcclusion;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT A GRAPH IS HANDED, as one struct.
//
// The four consumers call the medium with a CloudFieldParams, a CloudFieldSample and a position, and a
// generated medium reads members off all three. Gathering them into ONE named struct is what lets the
// graph's Cloud Sample node be a single emitted statement whose output pins are member reads — the pin
// NAME is the member name, so the emitter has no table of pin-to-expression mappings to keep level with
// the node catalogue. A pin renamed without renaming the member is then a GLSL error naming the member,
// which is the loudest failure available here.
struct CloudGraphSample
{
    vec3  PositionKm;
    vec3  WindPositionKm;
    float Profile;
    float DetailType;
    float DetailFactor;
    float DensityScale;
    float ExtinctionFactor;

    // 0 on a march that accumulates radiance, 1 on one that accumulates optical depth. See CLOUD_RAY_VIEW
    // in Common/CloudField.glslh for which of the four consumers is which. It is the author's lever for
    // the thing Epic calls ShadowSampleDistance: `mix( expensive, cheap, ShadowRay )` in the Density or
    // Extinction output buys back the ten-to-thirty samples a shadow quadrature spends per lit sample.
    float ShadowRay;

    // WHICH OF THE LAYER'S FOUR `.dcnv` VOLUMES THE WINNING SPECIES' EDGE IS CUT FROM, 0..3.
    //
    // WITHOUT IT AN AUTHORED MEDIUM CANNOT SAY "THE SAME VOLUME, ERODED DIFFERENTLY". A graph that writes
    // its own density could call CloudDefaultDensity and inherit the shipped erosion whole, or build a
    // field out of nothing — but the whole population of sculpted noise volumes a project owns was
    // unreachable from a canvas, because the one number that names them travelled only inside this file.
    // Handed out here it goes into the Cloud Noise Volume node, which is the other half: a member with no
    // node to consume it would be an integer an artist can wire nowhere, which is the dead knob the
    // contract refuses in the same breath as a stub.
    //
    // float AND NOT int, because this palette has one numeric pin type and a second one would exist for
    // this single value. CloudNoiseSlotOf above converts it back, rounding and clamping, so a slot that
    // survived a Lerp still names the volume the author meant.
    float NoiseSlot;
};

CloudGraphSample CloudGraphSampleAt( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudGraphSample s;
    s.PositionKm = positionKm;
    // THE WIND-SHIFTED POSITION, which is the frame the modelling volume was BAKED in — the same
    // subtraction the density chain above makes on its own way into the noise, and now literally the same
    // function. A graph that wants structure to travel with the weather uses this one; a graph that wants
    // it nailed to the world uses PositionKm.
    s.WindPositionKm   = CloudDefaultWindPositionKm( params, positionKm );
    s.Profile          = field.Profile;
    s.DetailType       = field.DetailType;
    s.DetailFactor     = field.DetailFactor;
    s.DensityScale     = field.DensityScale;
    s.ExtinctionFactor = field.ExtinctionFactor;
    // THE ONE MEMBER THAT COMES FROM THE MARCH RATHER THAN FROM THE FIELD, and the only reason it is
    // reachable at all: the caller's own params carry it, so a graph can tell a shadow quadrature from
    // the eye without any of the five entry points growing an argument.
    s.ShadowRay = params.ShadowRay;
    // WIDENED, NOT CONVERTED FOR CONVENIENCE. The producer decided this index; nothing here may reinterpret
    // it, so the only arithmetic is the widening that lets it travel on a Float pin.
    s.NoiseSlot = float( field.NoiseSlot );
    return s;
}

#endif // CLOUD_MEDIUM_DEFAULT_GLSLH
